Quoting  Janis Papanagnou  <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com>:
>If I have to wish for the "survival" kit that has been mentioned
>elsewhere in the thread, MR, reflection, conflict, what choices do
>I have?

Well, leave out conflict. Since my first ascension I have used conflict
twice, and one of those was because I was pressed for time in the closing
hours of a tournament. Conflict is manifestly not at all necessary; we see
lots of conflictless ascensions.

You pick MR. MR is vital (yes, I know people have ascended without it, but
it is a crapshoot); reflection is merely extremely useful except in the
sole case of black dragons. The odds are very good - if you don't make
mistakes, the lack of reflection is going to be a nuisance, not a killer.

[There is some chance it will be a killer, but I still don't think you can
have a roguelike that is all three of sufficiently random, sufficiently
challenging, contains no unfair deaths. That said, I fully applaud the
Spork approach of eliminating resistance-hole instadeaths, and I think it
makes it easier to write a game where relying on found armour not wishes
is viable.]

So... yes. A single wish lets you fix the one resistance hole, MR, which
is overwhelmingly likely to kill you. Isn't that enough?

>Or shall I proceed any game with the above kit
>because "you cannot lose such a game"? (Which isn't even true, IME.)

Obviously you _can_ lose a game with MR and reflection, but once you've
got MR and reflection (and the AC commensurate with one of them being a
DSM), the game is yours to lose whereas before it was yours to win. If you
lose such a game, you ought to be able to see that you made a bad mistake.
